WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.383
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.383 --> 00:00:12.178
One of the great things that
culture and art is capable of

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:14.264
is actually giving
a physical language

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:17.851
to something that is, otherwise,
highly mediated or abstract,

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:20.270
meaning that something
like climate change

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:23.940
is very difficult to understand
because I can't touch it.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:24.733
I can't measure it.

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:25.525
It's not like a--

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.444
it's not like a ball in my hand.

00:00:27.444 --> 00:00:28.945
It was actually,
for the first time,

00:00:28.945 --> 00:00:33.033
possible to sail the Northwest
Passage on the western side

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:35.702
of the North Pole,
connecting the two oceans

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:37.620
and shortening the
way of sailing.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.831
And it's been long time
debated whether this

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:41.666
will ever, ever be possible.

00:00:41.666 --> 00:00:44.002
And now, with global warming
and the melting of the ice,

00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:46.087
it suddenly is possible.

00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:47.714
And, in that sense,
I thought this was

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.632
a nice topic I'm interested in.

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:52.761
And the fact that, when
you see satellite pictures

00:00:52.761 --> 00:00:56.389
of the ocean, airplane pictures
of this particular ocean,

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:58.183
you see the ice
scattered around,

00:00:58.183 --> 00:01:01.269
and you realize that,
these white ice sheets,

00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:04.064
they look like small islands
or small pieces of land.

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:05.690
And, if you wanted
to cross, you'll

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.068
have to jump from
one to the other.

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:09.986
And that had interested me.

00:01:09.986 --> 00:01:12.072
I had actually made drawings
of that in my studio,

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:14.157
and I was just
interested in the fact

00:01:14.157 --> 00:01:17.952
that a lot of what we
know as being stable

00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:19.120
has become unstable.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.622
And a lot of the
things that we take

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:23.666
for granted as out of
the reach of change

00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:24.793
are, in fact, now changing.

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:30.507
So the world, as we know it,
has become a lot more relative.

00:01:30.507 --> 00:01:33.718
And, with regards to the whole
sort of idea of nanotechnology,

00:01:33.718 --> 00:01:37.013
I thought, OK, this is also
interesting because, in a way,

00:01:37.013 --> 00:01:42.268
nano is about making what we
could not see before visible

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:45.563
or making the invisible visible.

00:01:45.563 --> 00:01:50.193
As an artist, I'm trying to
sort out, well, what kind of art

00:01:50.193 --> 00:01:53.488
or what type of artistic
interventions in the world

00:01:53.488 --> 00:01:57.325
can I do to bring about a more
action-driven relationship

00:01:57.325 --> 00:01:58.868
with the climate
or an opportunity

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:03.206
for you to see, well, turning
your knowledge into action

00:02:03.206 --> 00:02:05.917
is actually something that is
a little easier than I thought

00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:06.417
it would be.

00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:08.795
And, actually, the
climate is not as abstract

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:09.838
as I thought it would be.

00:02:09.838 --> 00:02:11.131
It's right here in front of me.

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:13.299
I can touch it, and
it is understandable.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.009
And it is not like big science.

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:19.097
It's quite specific and so on.

00:02:19.097 --> 00:02:20.723
I think that art
alone should not

00:02:20.723 --> 00:02:24.978
be the one who communicates
the explicified version of what

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.438
climate science is, but it
is one of the great things

00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:30.316
that I think is exciting
to work with, as an artist,

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:36.114
is to make things, which we
know explicit as something

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:38.950
as we feel, giving
it you could call it

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.703
an emotional narrative,
besides the sort

00:02:41.703 --> 00:02:44.664
of data-driven,
data-driven narrative.

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:48.042
The artwork here came
out of my interest

00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:51.379
in various other artworks
to work with geometry

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:53.548
and these more random shapes.

00:02:53.548 --> 00:02:57.552
You could say these sort of
puddles or pools or ice sheets.

00:02:57.552 --> 00:02:59.512
And I am actually, in
this case, particularly

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:03.266
interested in the
fact, when you look up,

00:03:03.266 --> 00:03:05.351
you obviously see the
reflection of the ground

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.269
because it's a mirror.

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:07.937
And you look up, and
you see the ground.

00:03:07.937 --> 00:03:09.898
And, to that extent,
it is actually

00:03:09.898 --> 00:03:14.485
like a ceiling, which has these
small islands, if you want,

00:03:14.485 --> 00:03:15.069
of the ground.

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:16.654
And I've worked with
that a few times,

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:19.490
but you could also see
them as stepping stones.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:24.829
Should we be in a situation
where the South Pole melts--

00:03:24.829 --> 00:03:27.373
and the chances are significant.

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.334
That is not just
some crazy idea.

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:30.335
It actually is.

00:03:30.335 --> 00:03:33.171
That probably means that half of
Boston is completely underwater

00:03:33.171 --> 00:03:35.381
and not just a little bit,
like literally underwater.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.300
And going around
on the campus is

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.470
likely to be jumping from one
stepping stone to the other.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:41.930
So, when I talk
about these things,

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.642
I talk about the kind of
way that a space contains

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:49.437
not just us, but also our
action and our ability

00:03:49.437 --> 00:03:51.856
to interact and share things.

00:03:51.856 --> 00:03:55.068
And I think art can be
a part of that equation

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:58.947
in artistic
interventions and amplify

00:03:58.947 --> 00:04:03.159
what makes the space better,
amplify the fact that we should

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:07.080
be open-minded to the extent
of understanding that spaces

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.499
actually have social
potential as well.

00:04:09.499 --> 00:04:12.377
And, sometimes, you
need a language.

00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:16.339
So you need sort of a quality
in a space, which simply

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:21.219
triggers that sort of social or
that generous type of activity.

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:24.555
[MUSIC PLAYING]

